-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Why this licence and not MIT
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is deliberately restrictive because selling the plugin is still an option.
Under a permissive licence such as MIT, anyone could legally take the build,
upload it somewhere, and give it away - and you would have no standing to
object. That is not a hypothetical in the Minecraft plugin market.

The beta testing grant above is the narrow exception: it lets people run it and
report back, which is the only way to find out whether the thing works, without
giving away the right to redistribute or sell it.

If you would rather open-source it, MIT or Apache-2.0 are the usual choices and
swapping this file is all it takes - but note that anything already published
under a permissive licence stays that way for whoever has it.

This is a plain-language starting point written by a programmer, not a lawyer,
and it is not legal advice. If real money starts flowing through this, get an
actual licence reviewed.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party components
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

TickTriage bundles no third-party code. It compiles against, but does not
redistribute:

  paper-api        (Paper / PaperMC)          - GPL-3.0 / MIT, compileOnly
  worldguard       (EngineHub)                - LGPL-3.0,     compileOnly
  griefprevention  (TechFortress)             - MIT,          compileOnly

These are provided by the server at runtime and are not shipped in the jar.
Confirm each project's current terms before you publish.
